AB  - Privacy concerns are a major barrier to the adoption of predictive analytics in healthcare, particularly when diagnostic tasks require computation on sensitive patient data. Performing such computations in plaintext can expose private health information, especially in outsourced or cloud-based environments. To address this problem, this paper presents a secure blood pressure abnormality detection using the Cheon–Kim–Kim–Song (CKKS) homomorphic encryption scheme, which supports secure computation on encrypted real-valued inputs. A logistic regression model was trained in plaintext on a real-world medical dataset, with preprocessing including missing value imputation and min-max normalization. The trained model parameters were reused for encrypted inference, where test inputs were encrypted, followed by secure dot product computation and a polynomial approximated sigmoid activation. Decrypted predictions were compared with ground truth labels. Results show that encrypted inference closely matches plaintext performance, achieving over 73% accuracy under the CKKS (8192) configuration, along with strong precision, recall, and ROC AUC scores. These findings confirm the feasibility of CKKS-based encrypted inference for medical prediction tasks, offering a practical solution for privacy preserving clinical analytics.
