AB  - We present a privacy-preserving machine learning (PPML) model for distributed healthcare analytics, with emphasis of prediction on clinical duration. We used a clinical dataset and ensembled 1-D CNNs of each of 50 workstations as hospitals. For sequence like data, we used cumulative patient touchpoints and diagnostic assay counts. Then for encryption of data, we used a secure private loader which uses NumPy byte serialization and AES-256 CFB encryption with per node unique keys. For security purposes, we used Shamir's Secret Sharing (SSS), Garbled Circuits (GC) and Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) for the data. Also, in Cheon-Kim-Kim-Song-based (CKKS), a noise issue was reported. In the term “Noise Explosion”, as we multiply encrypted data, the noise quadratically increases. For example - if we start with 100, then grow linearly to 500, then exponentially from ($\mathbf{1. 0} \times \mathbf{1 0}^{\wedge} 7$ to $5.0 \times \mathbf{1 0}^{\wedge}$ 7). The bootstrapping mechanism is necessary for resetting noise levels before they reach the $\mathbf{1 0}^{\boldsymbol{\wedge}} \mathbf{8}$ noise threshold, leading to parity flip errors and encryption failures. Although there is a significant time overhead when evaluating an operation with FHE (4.4024s vs. 0.6287 s in encryption), the ensemble achieves a combined accuracy of 77.80%, beating the average accuracy of individual nodes (71.11%). Such a PPML solution is ideal for enabling the “Clinical Trust” model as the ensemble predicts “High Trust” predictions in 42.4% of cases.
ER  - 

