AB  - The growing reliance on machine learning (ML) in healthcare raises critical concerns regarding data privacy and security. While federated learning (FL) enables collaborative model training without centralized data sharing, it remains vulnerable to inference attacks through model gradients and unauthorized access by third parties. To address these challenges, this work proposes a privacy-preserving federated learning framework that integrates homomorphic encryption (HE) with logistic regression for secure medical data analysis. In the proposed approach, local model parameters are encrypted using homomorphic schemes before aggregation on a centralized server, ensuring that neither raw data nor model updates are exposed. Experimental evaluation with BFV, BGV, and CKKS encryption schemes demonstrates that the framework achieves competitive accuracy ($\gt 85 \%$) while safeguarding patient information. Among the tested schemes, BGV provided the highest accuracy ($\sim 89.81 \%$), whereas BFV achieved optimal training efficiency. The results highlight that combining federated learning with homomorphic encryption can effectively balance model performance and data confidentiality, making it a promising solution for privacy-preserving healthcare applications.
ER  - 

