AB  - The convergence of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and the Internet of Things (IoT) has revolutionized modern healthcare by enabling real-time disease monitoring, intelligent diagnostics, and personalized treatment strategies. However, despite considerable advancements, existing AI-IoT healthcare frameworks often remain disease-specific, data-limited, and computationally intensive, thereby restricting their scalability and adaptability across diverse health conditions and deployment environments. This paper explores state-of-art in AI-IoT based disease diagnosis models and focuses on addressing various challenges such as lack of scalable AI-IOT framework for multi-disease diagnosis, accurate real-time prediction incorporating lightweight and edgecompatible AI Architecture, the integration of heterogeneous biomedical data modalities such as wearable sensor data, medical imaging, electronic health records (EHRs), and audio cues into unified diagnostic engines, incorporation of privacy-preserving mechanisms including federated learning, secure aggregation, homomorphic encryption, and block chain technologies The paper analyses recent contribution based on core design principles, data modalities, computational performance, and privacy guarantees. The analysis reveals significant gaps in multimodal data fusion at the edge, holistic privacy-preserving model integration, and the real-time deployment of scalable and adaptive AI frameworks in clinical environments. To bridge these gaps, we propose a roadmap toward a modular, multimodal, and privacy-aware smart health infrastructure that leverages edge AI and federated learning. This paper aims to guide future research and development efforts toward building robust, interpretable, and regulation-compliant AI-IoT ecosystems for next-generation smart healthcare.
