AB  - The sensitive sector like medical care are growing rapidly, it is critical to maintain data confidentiality and integrity when using artificial intelligence (AI) in computing. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs), a piece of deep learning model, have shown impressive results in applications like image classification. However, there are significant risks associated with exposing raw data to central servers when using traditional centralized model training techniques. In highly regulated spaces such as healthcare, this model of computation is no longer an option due to data privacy issues and legislation such as GDPR and HIPAA. To overcome this vulnerability, we propose an end-to-end image classification system computed in an encrypted environment based on Homomorphic Encryption. It helps in computation on encrypted data without decrypting it, hence maintaining privacy even at inference and training phases. The implementation relies on the Cheon-Kim-Kim-Song (CKKS) encryption scheme implemented by the TenSEAL library, enabling approximate arithmetic on real numbers. A ResNet18 deep learning model pretrained on ImageN et and fine-tuned on a chest X-ray dataset is used as the base classifier. The model is adapted to run on encrypted feature vectors, allowing inference in an encrypted environment. Chest X-ray images from four classes-COVID-19, pneumonia, tuberculosis, and normal-make up the dataset used. This experiment demonstrates that homomorphically encrypted image classification is feasible with negligible performance and latency trade-offs. The model's classification accuracy is about 88%, and data confidentiality is 100% guaranteed. The deployment of secure AI models to privacy-critical applications, where data leakage can be catastrophic, like AI-aided radiology, smart diagnostics, and remote health care, is made possible by this work.
ER  - 

