AB  - Privacy-preserving medical imaging through Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) is critical for secure data processing but is severely constrained by the massive computational overhead of encrypted domain operations. This study evaluates a privacy-preserving medical diagnostic system for mammograms using an optimized, FHE-native Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and a high-performance compilation environment. We demonstrate a critical precision-latency trade-off: while a highprecision configuration achieves $89.68 \%$ accuracy, it necessitates an inference latency of $\mathbf{3 4}$ minutes. Conversely, a 3-bit quantized model maintains a suitable accuracy for secondary screening of $86.04 \%$ while reducing execution time to just 74.14 seconds. These results characterize the feasibility of “FHE-first” architectural designs, providing a framework for high-throughput, secure oncological data evaluation. This study investigates the structural and precision-based optimizations required to balance evaluation reliability with the hardware constraints of modern FHE compilers.
ER  - 

