AB  - As Internet of Things (IoT)-powered healthcare systems are rapidly adopted, wearable sensors and smart devices generate large volumes of patient data, which can be used for immediate disease tracking, but the conventional diagnostic framework encounters issues of fragmentation of IoT integration, excessive classes distribution, and a risk of compromised patient data privacy. The intention of this study is to design a powerful, precise yet privacy-focused disease diagnosis model based on the use of the IoT sensor fusion data to make multi-class predictions. The proposed methodology uses a combination of demographic, symptomatic, and wearable sensor data, SMOTE to manage the class imbalance, and trains a hybrid stacking ensemble of XGBoost and Random Forest base learners with multinomial logistic regression as a meta-learner, and ensures privacy via importance-driven feature selection and local differential privacy (ε=1.0, δ=1e5); homomorphic encryption is also simulated to evaluate the feasibility of fully encrypted processing. These findings show excellent performance at 99.66 percent accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score, strong confusion matrices, and recognition of the best IoT predictors (body temperature, heart rate, oxygen saturation) and are superior to available models such as SVM, CNN, LSTM, and Random Forest. This framework provides a scalable, clinically sound, and safe IoT diagnostic system, which has provided dual zero-loss privacy mechanisms, and established a new standard of disease diagnosis, serves as the basis of future expansion to other datasets, multi-disease prediction and privacy-preserving implementations.
ER  - 

