AB  - The rapid expansion of genomic data and bioinformatics research has intensified the need for secure and collaborative data analysis while maintaining patient confidentiality. Conventional centralized machine learning approaches expose sensitive genome sequence data to privacy risks and regulatory challenges. To overcome these limitations, this paper introduces a novel Hybrid Adaptive Federated Learning Framework for Privacy-Preserving Genome Analytics (HAFL-GA). The HAFL-GA framework uniquely integrates adaptive client weighting, multi-level differential privacy, and a Genomic Feature Alignment (GFA) module to enhance model accuracy and privacy simultaneously. The adaptive client weighting dynamically adjusts the contribution of each participating institution based on data quality and heterogeneity. At the same time, the GFA module aligns genomic feature distributions across nodes, minimizing bias in federated updates. Secure aggregation and lightweight homomorphic encryption are employed to safeguard model parameters during communication. Experimental results on benchmark genome sequence datasets show that HAFL-GA achieves up to 7% higher prediction accuracy and 30% lower communication cost compared to traditional federated learning models, without compromising data privacy. This demonstrates its robustness and scalability for real-world bioinformatics applications, including variant classification and disease susceptibility analysis. In conclusion, HAFL-GA provides an innovative and efficient approach to privacy-preserving genomic data analytics, fostering secure cross-institutional collaboration in biomedical research. The proposed HAFL-GA achieved 94.1% accuracy, 0.24 loss, 320 MB communication cost, and 97% privacy resistance $(\varepsilon=2.3)$, proving superior efficiency, accuracy, and privacy preservation.
ER  - 

