AB  - In sensitive fields like healthcare, machine learning adoption is limited by privacy and security concerns. Federated Learning (FL) exposes vulnerabilities through inference attacks on model updates, whereas centralized approaches run the risk of data breaches. For detecting breast cancer, this paper presents a privacy-preserving framework that combines FL with CKKS homomorphic encryption. Hospitals can employ our strategy to locally train logistic regression models, encrypt weight updates with CKKS, and aggregate them without decryption. The system, which was implemented with PyTorch and OpenFHE, maintains data confidentiality throughout and achieves accuracy of 80% on the Wisconsin Breast Cancer Dataset. The framework ensures compliance with HIPAA and GDPR regulations while introducing only 22% less computational overhead than plain FL. Encryption efficiency will be the primary focus of future research, as will data distributions outside of IID.
