AB  - As quantum computing advances toward practical deployment, healthcare faces a dual challenge: enabling powerful computational analytics while guaranteeing rigorous patient data privacy. Existing classical approaches, particularly Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE), suffer from prohibitive performance costs and computational security assumptions vulnerable to quantum adversaries. This paper presents QPrivMed, a novel privacy-preserving framework for medical data computation to Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) devices. QPrivMed couples the Quantum One-Time Pad (QOTP) with selective homomorphic evaluation over Clifford circuits, achieving information-theoretic security for targeted clinical tasks without fault-tolerant hardware. Validated on 8-qubit IBM Quantum processors, the framework achieves 94.7% average circuit fidelity across binary medical classification scenarios, while outperforming classical FHE by a factor of 4.4× in execution time for equivalent Clifford workloads. Security analysis confirms zero mutual information leakage and tamper detection with probability ≥ 1 − 2−λ. This work bridges quantum cryptographic theory and practical healthcare data processing, establishing a scalable foundation for quantum-secured medical analytics while providing an honest assessment of current limitations and a realistic deployment timeline extending to the 2030.
ER  - 

