AB  - Although digital technologies make key contributions to healthcare, concerns over their breaches have been raised, particularly in health-related data such as patient records. Cyber-attacks, data leakage, and unauthorised access could jeopardise medical records in centralised networks by undermining patient confidentiality, healthcare delivery systems, and public trust in digital tools. This study presents an integrated healthcare data management framework comprising federated learning (FL), blockchain infrastructure, homomorphic encryption (HE), and privacy-preserving smart contracts. On the other hand, the blockchain represents a secure but immutable ledger system; FL enables healthcare institutions to collaborate to develop machine learning (ML) models without exchanging raw patient data. The HE secures data privacy throughout the process of data encryption as it allows computations to be made using encrypted data inputs. The healthcare institutions use smart contracts that enforce the access control and data sharing. In this vein, privacy preservation is demonstrated through the federated averaging (FedAvg) algorithm to predict heart arrhythmia with the Random Forest model in an FL setup to describe the impact of healthcare data on healthcare information security. The proposed framework is designed to operate in the presence of potential untrusted participants with a focus on privacy assurance, data governance and auditability in collaborative healthcare learning.
ER  - 

